se  amongst Nina's songs is you know a most  delicious dilemma you know at the end of  the day we chose songs by you know their  their narrative value that we use songs  to advance the narrative not just you  know because of their purely aesthetic  qualities or how much I might just love  a song and you know that we started off  from it with an archival point of view  on this film we wanted to find as much  Nina as we could and one of the great  gifts was the audio we found all the  interviews which started as early as 59  and go through the late 90s of Nina  telling her story to various  interviewers or Co biographers when she  was working on her her memoir and so we  had a wealth of Nina talking about her  own life stuff that you know had never  been collected or listened to except by  those interviewers and that was  wonderful you know that was an  extraordinary gift to really listen to  her processing her own the questions  about her own life that interviewers  were asking her there were moments that  you recreated when she's a little girl  playing piano in the church and walking  along those dangerous railroad tracks  how did you come to decide that was the  way to tell that part of the story well  we found wonderful archival material by  a documentarian working in the South and  the 30s and 40s and 50s named H Lee  Waters who shot everyday life in small  southern towns and so I felt like we  could paint a picture of Nina's  environment as a child with that footage  except this little girl playing the  piano so we chose to kind of you know I  studied a chilly waters material and  then we chose to shoot a little girl  playing the piano to kind of put Nina in  that environment that we found there and  working with the family working with the  estate was there veto-power was what  what what power was put into your hands  is the filmmaker  well I had ultimately the final cut of  the film and Lisa the Nina's daughter  was extraordinary I mean I met her when  I first pitched myself as a director for  the project  I didn't talk to her until you know I  well and we had a great meeting and we  talked and she told me some of her  stories and then I met her again nine  months later when I spent a day  interviewing her and then I showed her  the film you know another six or seven  eight months later before the film went  to Sundance and she hadn't she gave no  comment she had no notes and she said  you know mommy's story's been told the  truth is out there and I can I can move  on and that was the that was her  interaction with the film I mean and  that was that was all of it in terms of  any kind of yeah there were there was  never of a veto of anything and for her  daughter did her daughter ask anything  of you the question of I want this film  to tell this about my mother I mean I  think the domestic violence that went on  in her home that was important for her  to express that and have that be  corroborated by others who I spoke to I  think that was also important to her I  think it's in there I think what she  talked about and you know what I put in  the film is it was what was it that was  what was important to her to express  about her mom what what influence do you  think she's had nowadays on music it's  huge I mean the artists today who kind  of give props to Nina Simone or feel  like they're standing on her shoulders  are some of the greatest artists and you  can't and there's so many of them who  kind of have you know express support  and inspiration from her you know from  you know people like Alicia Keys to  people like Lauryn Hill to John Legend  to comment I mean there's a real Bob  Dylan you know I mean there's just a  love and a regard for her amongst  musicians and performers that's pretty  stunning as a documentarian you can  finish the film but do you ever leave  the subject does it stay with you no and  the most wonderful thing about Nina is  you know you can just I can listen to  her all the time and have this 